// ============================================================================
// Hunt: Help-desk impersonation to AD CS abuse — ESC1 and ESC8
// (Microsoft Threat Intelligence, October 30, 2026) — Teams
// impersonation, Quick Assist access, and certificate abuse that
// turns a single workstation compromise into domain-admin
// certificates, DCSync, and data theft
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1566 (Phishing — Teams help-desk impersonation),
//              T1219 (Remote Access Software — Quick Assist),
//              T1553 (Subvert Trust Controls — AD CS certificate
//              abuse: ESC1 template abuse, ESC8 web-enrollment relay),
//              T1187 (Forced Authentication — PetitPotam over EFSRPC),
//              T1649 (Steal or Forge Kerberos Tickets — PKINIT ticket
//              from a relayed domain-controller certificate),
//              T1003.006 (OS Credential Dumping: DCSync)
// Data: Microsoft Defender — DeviceProcessEvents, DeviceNetworkEvents
//       and SecurityEvent (AD CS 4887, directory-service 4662,
//       logon 4624). Scope Variant 4 to your AD CS hosts before
//       triaging it.
// Write-up: https://amitvijayan.com/journal/articles/daily-cyber-threat-brief-october-31-2026.html
// What happened (Microsoft, October 30, 2026): in two intrusions,
//      attackers posed as IT support on Microsoft Teams —
//      organization-themed tenant names and support-oriented display
//      names helped the external contacts look familiar — persuaded
//      users to open Quick Assist and share connection codes, then
//      abused misconfigured Active Directory Certificate Services.
//      Neither intrusion exploited a Teams vulnerability, and
//      Microsoft attributes the activity to no specific actor.
//      After connecting, the attackers transferred archives into
//      Downloads folders, extracted payloads into KB-prefixed
//      directories, and launched portable Python, Bun, or Java
//      runtimes to survey the host and assess certificate services
//      over LDAPS and SMB.
//      Intrusion 1 (ESC1): a certificate template with broad
//      enrollment permissions, requester-supplied identity
//      information, and weak issuance controls let a low-privileged
//      account request authentication certificates for privileged
//      identities. The first wave targeted three users and
//      compromised one workstation, yielding certificates for three
//      highly privileged accounts; about 90 minutes after
//      disruption, a second wave targeted 16 users, compromised
//      another workstation, and secured certificates for eight more
//      privileged accounts. One privileged identity excluded from
//      automated containment later supported DCSync and data theft,
//      while Defender blocked more than 1,000 subsequent SMB
//      file-access attempts and additional logons.
//      Intrusion 2 (ESC8): attackers used PetitPotam over EFSRPC to
//      coerce domain-controller authentication and relayed the NTLM
//      authentication to inadequately protected AD CS Web Enrollment
//      (no HTTPS / no Extended Protection for Authentication),
//      obtaining a domain-controller certificate and a
//      ticket-granting ticket via Kerberos PKINIT. The customer
//      isolated the workstation and no further progression was
//      observed.
//      The durable lesson, per Microsoft: a password reset does not
//      revoke an issued certificate. Review AD CS template
//      permissions, web-enrollment protections, and containment
//      exclusions — not just the help-desk workflow that let the
//      attacker in. No hashes, IPs, or domains were published, so
//      every variant below is behavioral; baseline each one against
//      your own legitimate Quick Assist use, certificate issuance,
//      and DC replication before calling a hit compromise.
// ============================================================================
// -- Variant 1a: Quick Assist sessions — the entry point --
// Quick Assist is legitimate support tooling, so this variant is a
// triage list, not an alert: look for sessions outside support hours,
// sessions for users who never normally receive remote support, and
// any device that appears here and also in Variants 1b, 2, or 3.
DeviceProcessEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(14d)
| where FileName =~ "QuickAssist.exe"
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, AccountName, InitiatingProcessAccountName,
    ProcessCommandLine, FolderPath
| order by TimeGenerated desc
// -- Variant 1b: portable runtimes launched from KB-prefixed payload dirs --
// The one exact on-host pattern Microsoft published: archives
// transferred into Downloads, extracted into KB-prefixed
// directories, then portable Python, Bun, or Java runtimes launched
// from there to survey the host and assess certificate services.
// A runtime launched from Downloads\KB* has no legitimate reason to
// exist — check the device against Variant 1a first.
DeviceProcessEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(14d)
| where FolderPath has @"\Downloads\KB"
| where FileName in~ ("python.exe", "bun.exe", "java.exe")
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, AccountName, FileName, FolderPath,
    ProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessFileName
| order by TimeGenerated desc
// -- Variant 2: AD CS certificate issuance bursts (Event 4887) --
// ESC1 turns a low-privileged account into a privileged identity by
// issuing it an authentication certificate, so issuance volume per
// requesting account is the signal. Compare every hit against your
// privileged-account inventory and your list of ESC1-vulnerable
// templates (broad enrollment + requester-supplied subject +
// weak issuance controls): issuance to a privileged account from a
// requester that never normally enrolls is the case to open first.
// A password reset does not revoke an issued certificate — revoke
// suspect certificates, do not just reset the account.
SecurityEvent
| where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
| where EventID == 4887
| summarize Issuances = count(),
            FirstSeen = min(TimeGenerated),
            LastSeen = max(TimeGenerated)
    by Computer, SubjectUserName, TargetUserName
| order by Issuances desc
// -- Variant 3: DCSync — directory replication rights used by a user account --
// In intrusion 1, a privileged identity excluded from automated
// containment later supported DCSync. The replication extended
// rights are DS-Replication-Get-Changes (1131f6aa) and
// DS-Replication-Get-Changes-All (1131f6ad); legitimate DC-to-DC
// replication uses them too, so this variant excludes machine
// accounts (names ending in $) and surfaces user-account access.
// Any remaining hit is a privileged-account compromise until proven
// otherwise.
SecurityEvent
| where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
| where EventID == 4662
| where RenderedDescription has_any ("1131f6aa", "1131f6ad")
| where not(SubjectUserName endswith "$")
| project TimeGenerated, Computer, SubjectUserName, SubjectDomainName,
    TargetUserName, ObjectName, EventRecordId
| order by TimeGenerated desc
// -- Variant 4a: network logons to certificate-services hosts (ESC8 relay setup) --
// ESC8 needs a relayed NTLM authentication to reach AD CS Web
// Enrollment, so the precursor is a network logon (type 3) to a
// certificate-services host from a source that does not normally
// authenticate there. Scope Computer to your AD CS / CA hosts before
// triaging; first-seen source IPs go to the top of the list.
SecurityEvent
| where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
| where EventID == 4624 and LogonType == 3
| summarize Logons = count(),
            FirstSeen = min(TimeGenerated),
            LastSeen = max(TimeGenerated)
    by Computer, IpAddress, TargetUserName
| order by FirstSeen desc
// -- Variant 4b: SMB file-access bursts from a single device --
// In intrusion 1, Defender blocked more than 1,000 subsequent SMB
// file-access attempts after the certificate abuse. A device that
// suddenly opens an order of magnitude more SMB sessions than its
// baseline — especially right after a Variant 1b or Variant 2 hit on
// the same device — is the post-compromise sweep to investigate.
DeviceNetworkEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(14d)
| where RemotePort == 445
| summarize Connections = count(),
            Targets = dcount(RemoteIP),
            FirstSeen = min(TimeGenerated),
            LastSeen = max(TimeGenerated)
    by DeviceName, bin(TimeGenerated, 1h)
| order by Connections desc
